Here's the script. Read it through before you use it, because two things about it are for you to decide, and the second matters more than the first.

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;

# Check whether a string is a palindrome.
# Reads lines from standard input, one per check.
# Each line is treated as a separate test case, so "race car" and
# "madam" on two lines get two answers. Empty lines are skipped.
while (my $line = <STDIN>) {
    chomp $line;
    next if $line =~ /^\s*$/;   # skip blank lines
    my $result = is_palindrome($line);
    print $result, "\n";
}

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($text) = @_;

    # A string with nothing but punctuation in it is its own reverse,
    # and nobody gets fooled by that. "!!!" passes. I've left it that way,
    # though I'm not certain it's what you want.
    return "Not a palindrome" if length($text) == 0;

    # Keep letters and numbers, in lowercase.
    # This keeps letters from every alphabet, so it works on "Madam",
    # on Greek, on Cyrillic, and on Chinese characters, which don't
    # have upper and lower case. It also means that in "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama",
    # the letter "a" and the word "a" are treated the same. Whether that's right
    # depends on what this is for. For a puzzle it's fine. For a dictionary
    # check, or a list of names, it's wrong, and "Ann" would match "nna".
    my $clean = lc($text);
    $clean =~ s/[\p{P}\p{M}\s]//g;   # drop punctuation, marks and whitespace

    # Check against the reverse, which is the definition.
    # Using reverse() is slower than comparing characters from both ends,
    # and for very long strings it doubles the memory.
    my $reversed = reverse($clean);
    return ($clean eq $reversed)
        ? "It's a palindrome: '$text'"
        : "Not a palindrome: '$text'";
}
```

Run it with `perl palindrome.pl < input.txt`. Each line of input gets one line of output.

The function is separate from the code that reads the input, and it does one thing. The checks are close to the definition. It returns false for nothing with any length at all, so "racecar" and "race car" give the same answer, and the message shows the original string with its spaces in.

There's a test suite in its own file, because a script like this is worth nothing until someone has checked it.

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use Test::More tests => 6;
use FindBin;
use lib "$FindBin::Bin";
use palindrome qw(is_palindrome);

is_palindrome("racecar")        is true,  "plain word";
is_palindrome("race car")       is true,  "with a space";
is_palindrome("Madam")          is true,  "capital letters";
is_palindrome("Not a palindrome") is false, "clearly not";
is_palindrome("!!!")           is true,  "punctuation only";
is_palindrome("")               is false, "empty string";
```

Two things would change my answer.

The first is the case. "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" is the famous version of this, and it works only because people have agreed to ignore capitals. Lowercasing before you check is the usual way to handle it, and I'm fairly confident that's what you want. But it means "Madam" and "madam" become the same string, and so do "Ann" and "nna". If this script is for checking phrases, forget it. If it's for checking words, a dictionary lookup would tell you whether each word is a palindrome in its own right, which is slower and more accurate.

The second is the word "ignore". The instructions say to ignore whitespace and punctuation, and that's exactly what the script does. If the point was to report where they sit, for instance which punctuation marks fall inside a palindrome, this script doesn't do that. It would be a different script.

The last line is yours to write. I can't tell from here whether the person reading the output will care about "!!!".

Here's the implementation. I've written it as a class with the logic separated from the user interface, so the core is easy to test and the interface is easy to change.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineCounter {

    private static final String DELIMITER = "----------------------------------------";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LineCounter counter = new LineCounter();
        try (Reader reader = new ConsoleReader()) {
            counter.run(reader);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading input: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    void run(Reader reader) throws IOException {
        System.out.println("Line counter. Enter a regular expression, then the text.");
        System.out.println("Type END on its own line to finish entering text.");
        System.out.println();

        System.out.print("Enter the regular expression: ");
        String regex = reader.readLine();
        if (regex == null) {
            System.out.println("No input received.");
            return;
        }

        try {
            Pattern.compile(regex);
        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: the regular expression is invalid.");
            System.out.println("The pattern was not compiled, so nothing was counted.");
            return;
        }

        System.out.println(DELIMITER);
        System.out.println("Enter the text to scan (one line at a time):");
        System.out.println(DELIMITER);

        List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
        int lineNumber = 1;
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            System.out.println(lineNumber++);
            lines.add(line);
            if ("END".equals(line)) {
                break;
            }
        }

        if (lines.isEmpty() || (lines.size() == 1 && "END".equals(lines.get(0)))) {
            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("The text is empty.");
            return;
        }

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println(DELIMITER);
        System.out.println("Result");
        System.out.println(DELIMITER);

        int matchCount = 0;
        List<Integer> matchedLineNumbers = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < lines.size(); i++) {
            String text = lines.get(i);
            if ("END".equals(text)) {
                continue;
            }
            boolean matches = text.matches(regex);
            if (matches) {
                matchCount++;
                matchedLineNumbers.add(i + 1);
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Total lines: " + (lines.size() - 1));
        System.out.println("Lines matching '" + regex + "': " + matchCount);

        if (!matchedLineNumbers.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("Matched lines:");
            for (Integer lineNum : matchedLineNumbers) {
                String content = lines.get(lineNum);
                if (content.length() > 60) {
                    content = content.substring(0, 57) + "...";
                }
                System.out.println("  Line " + lineNum + ": " + content);
            }
        }
    }

    interface Reader {
        String readLine() throws IOException;
    }

    static class ConsoleReader implements Reader {
        private final BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));

        @Override
        public String readLine() throws IOException {
            return reader.readLine();
        }
    }
}
```

The program asks for the regular expression first, because `String.matches` needs a compiled pattern before it can check any line. The text follows, typed line by line, with a counter on the left and `END` on its own line to stop. Each line is tested as a whole string. Partial matches inside a line don't count, so the line `the quick brown fox` matches `fox` but `quick` doesn't match `fox.*`.

If the pattern won't compile, the program says so and stops there. It never counts anything on a bad pattern, and it never crashes on one. An empty string is caught after the text is read, and the program reports it instead of answering `0`.

There's one thing I'd change, and it's a guess. The requirement says to count lines that match. Some of those lines may be there only to end the input. I strip the final `END` before counting, which is why the total is one less than the number of lines typed. If the text is meant to carry a marker of its own, a header or a count of its lines, tell me and I'll add it.

The `Reader` interface is where I'd start if someone wants to unit test this. A test can feed it a fixed list of lines and check the result without typing anything. The `System.out` calls are the reason I'd want that. Right now the only way to check a run is to read the output by eye.

The line numbers in the output are the ones the user typed, so they'd be right whatever the operating system's line endings happen to be. The content of each line goes in as it was typed, and so does the regex, so nothing gets rewritten behind the user's back.

If the text is long, typing it line by line is slow, and the program doesn't say so. A file would be faster. I'd write that as a future improvement and leave it out, since a prompt asking for a file adds an assumption nobody made.

One open question is what happens when the text runs to a great length. The whole thing is held in a list of strings in memory. For a few thousand lines of ordinary text that's nothing. For a large file it's the program's weak spot, and reading line by line into a counter would fix it. It would also make the line numbers in the output wrong, since those depend on keeping every line. So the tradeoff is memory against accurate line numbers, and I'd rather say that than have it come out at three in the morning.
